Open a software project and choose **Code** beside **Docs**. The file list shows the snapshot that Home has accepted. Use the branch or tag picker to choose a version. A root README appears below the file list. Choose its filename to open it, or browse another folder or file. For a Markdown file, **Read** shows the formatted page and **Raw** shows its exact text.

## Clone a project

1. In the project's **Code** view, expand **Clone**. Copy the **Clone URL**.
2. Choose **Create read credential** if your Home offers it. Copy the credential while it is visible and note when it expires.
3. In a terminal, run the command below with your copied URL in place of \`CLONE_URL\`. When Git asks, enter \`stead\` as the username and paste the Git credential at the password prompt.

\`\`\`sh
git clone CLONE_URL my-project
cd my-project
git log -1 --oneline
\`\`\`

The characters you paste at the password prompt may stay invisible. Keep the credential out of URLs, shell commands, issues and screenshots. Your Urbit sign-in approves your browser session. The Git credential gives your Git client access to this repository.

## Check which version you have

In your clone, fetch updated references and inspect the latest commit on the remote branch. Substitute your branch name if the project uses a different one.

\`\`\`sh
git fetch origin
git log -1 --oneline origin/main
\`\`\`

Compare that commit with the one shown in **Code** for the same branch. Fetching leaves your working files alone. A branch can move when a new change is accepted. A commit ID identifies one exact version.

## Share a change

A read credential covers clone and fetch. You can still edit files and commit in your local clone. Ask the project maintainer which remote and review process accept contributions before pushing. The private pilot has verified clone and fetch. An authorized review and merge can be claimed only when the Home confirms them.

## If Git stops working

For an authentication error, check the username, the credential's expiry and your current project access. Your Git client may have cached an older password for this repository. For a connection error on the private pilot, check the private connection first. A project shows **Code** when it has a repository you can access.

This Handbook README is a reading example. Get the clone URL and credential from the project whose files you want.
`;var T0="019a0000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001",x_="019a0000-0000-7000-8000-000000000003",Ou="726566732f68656164732f6d61696e",C0="524541444d452e6d64",__="d9f3fd7188d9ba89e2aa935c4cd995e69e2b389b",N0="345276e439c94315fd446df7df64d2c33fd26954",bm="1791178cbe4cc9067ff83a4d7bf08ba59dcdde433d36d7de338fa3980874a8a6";function Vo(){return Response.json({error:"denied_or_not_found"},{status:403})}function Hu(e,t){return Object.keys(e).length===t.length&&t.every(n=>Object.hasOwn(e,n))}function E0(e,t){let n="/stead/api/code";if(e!==n&&e!=="/stead/api/code/browse")return;if(t===null||typeof t!="object"||Array.isArray(t))return Vo();let i=t;if(i.project_id!==T0)return Vo();if(e===n){let c="stead.code/1",l=["protocol","project_id","action"];return i.protocol!==c?Vo():i.action==="external-list"&&(Hu(i,l)||Hu(i,[...l,"work_id"])&&i.work_id===x_)?Response.json({protocol:c,status:"proposals",rows:"[]"}):Hu(i,l)?i.action==="status"?Response.json({protocol:c,status:"external",write:"no",manage:"no"}):i.action==="pack-view"?Response.json({protocol:c,status:"external",snapshot:bm,authority_epoch:"1",generation:"1",object_count:"3",request_id:"019a0000-0000-7000-8000-000000000009",accepted_at_ms:"1795651200000",head:Ou,refs:JSON.stringify({[Ou]:N0})}):Vo():Vo()}let o="stead.git-browse/1";if(!Hu(i,["protocol","project_id","snapshot","ref","path","view"])||i.protocol!==o||i.snapshot!==bm||i.ref!==Ou||!Array.isArray(i.path))return Vo();let r={protocol:o,project_id:T0,snapshot:bm,ref:Ou,path:i.path,commit:N0};return i.view==="tree"&&i.path.length===0?Response.json({...r,status:"tree",truncated:!1,entries:[{name:C0,kind:"file",oid:__}]}):i.view==="file"&&i.path.length===1&&i.path[0]===C0?Response.json({...r,status:"file",preview:"text",bytes:new TextEncoder().encode(ym).length,text:ym}):Vo()}var R0=`# Why Stead?

Stead keeps the task, the decision behind it and the change it produced together in a workspace your team runs on its own Urbit ship. When somebody else picks up the work, they can see what changed and why. Your writing stays in Markdown and your code stays in Git.

Say a customer finds a bug in the setup instructions. The developer fixing the code and the person rewriting the guide can work from the same task. The next person answering that customer can open the task, read the decision and follow the link to the corrected guide.

## When would I choose it?

- **GitHub or GitLab.** If the pull request already tells the whole story, you\u2019re in good shape. Stead earns its place when support, documentation or operations share responsibility for the outcome. A project can start with a task and a page and add a repository when it needs one.
- **Atlassian\u2019s Jira and Confluence.** Both connect issues and pages. Choose Stead when you want one workspace your team runs, where your ship controls membership and you can take the project\u2019s versioned writing and Git history with you.
- **A local folder.** It works well when the whole project is yours. Stead helps when you hand work to someone else. They can find the owner, the decision and the current page without asking you to piece the story together.
`;var z0=`# How Stead works

## Why put these things together?

Say the ticket is in Jira, the visitor guide is in Confluence, and the site that publishes the guide is in Git. You can paste links between all three. A month later someone still has to work out which version the ticket referred to, who approved the change and whether the published text actually says the same thing.

I\u2019d like to answer those questions by opening the project. In Stead, Work holds the request and its discussion. The guide has an address and a saved revision. If a change also touches software, Code can show the exact file at a particular commit. A team organizing an event can stop at the guide. A software team can follow the change into the repository.

## Follow a change

Say the Monday meeting moves to Wednesday. In **Work**, one item records the request, the discussion and what a reviewer needs to check. In **Docs**, the visitor guide has its own saved revision. A relationship connects them. Someone can open the task from its supporting page and see why that sentence changed. If the meeting time also changes while you are editing, the revision matters.

When you save, the browser sends a request to the project\u2019s **Home** with the revision you started from. Home checks your current access and that revision. If both checks pass, it records the change and returns a receipt. If somebody else saved first, you can open the current version, compare the two and decide what to keep. A request ID lets Home recognize a retry after an interrupted connection.

## What Urbit does here

A ship is a personal Urbit server with its own network identity. In the private pilot, one ship hosts Stead Home and another can approve a member\u2019s browser sign-in. The member approves the request on their identity ship. Urbit tells Home which ship actually sent the approval. Home matches that sender to an enrolled member and applies the project\u2019s permissions. The team can operate its own workspace while members keep their own identities. That\u2019s how two people looking at the same project link can have different access.

Home is a Hoon application running under Gall, Urbit\u2019s application system. It processes changes in order and keeps its state across restarts. That gives the team one place to settle what was accepted, even when several browsers or workers are involved. Reading a page sends the authorized content to your browser. Other members\u2019 ships don\u2019t need a copy of the whole project. [How Gall applications keep state](https://docs.urbit.org/build-on-urbit/userspace)

## What lives in Git, and what lives in Home

| When you open | What you are reading |
| --- | --- |
| A Work item | A structured record in Home. The title, assignment, state and discussion belong to the project. Project and personal views refer to the same item. |
| A Docs page | Markdown with a revision in an authorized collection. The collection\u2019s exported history can be opened and checked with Git tools. |
| A repository file | The bytes at a path in a Git commit. A branch can advance. The commit ID identifies the exact version you read. |
| Project access or a receipt | Home\u2019s record of who may act or which change it accepted. A Git clone carries repository content and history. Restoring the team\u2019s memberships and work requires a Home backup as well. |

There\u2019s a practical reason for keeping the writing in ordinary files. If you move away from Stead, you can recover a Docs collection, open its Markdown in another editor and inspect its history with Git. If you want to restore the whole workspace, you also need the Home state that explains who had access and why the team did the work.

On the private pilot, the Git worker serves a repository snapshot that Home has accepted. A member used ordinary Git to clone it, check its object history and fetch again. That same commit can be inspected in **Code**. For a team that just needs tasks and pages, **Work** and **Docs** stand on their own.

## Where agents fit

The design gives a worker a specific task, the input revisions it may read and a bounded way to propose a result. A local or hosted model can supply the reasoning. Home and the reviewer still decide what enters the project. For the meeting change, a useful result is the proposed Markdown edit against the exact page the worker read.

The pilot already supports member sign-in, project Work and Docs, and reading an accepted Git repository. The full agent handoff is still being tested. A model describing the right edit in a trial left the saved page untouched. The next milestone is a reviewer opening an actual proposal, checking the change and accepting it into the page\u2019s history.
`;var j0=`# Work and Docs

## Find something to do

Open **My work** to see items assigned to you. To see a team's queue, choose its project and open **Work**. Expand a status group and select a title for the description and discussion. Use **Filters** to narrow a long list.

In the Handbook project, open **Update the visitor guide**. The task includes completion criteria, a discussion and a link to the agent guide under **Relationships \u2192 Show links**.

## Try a small handoff

In a project where you can edit, create a Work item such as \u201CCheck the visitor guide.\u201D Include what to check, who should do it and how to tell that it is finished. A comment records a conversation. Set the status after the outcome is ready.

## Read or edit a page

Open **Docs**, choose a collection and select a page title. **Read** shows the saved Markdown. **Edit** lets an authorized member change the body, **Preview** shows the formatting and **Source** exposes the full Markdown. Save at Home, then reopen the page to confirm the revision. A private draft stays private until someone explicitly publishes it.

## Find it again

Use workspace **Search** for a phrase from the page or Work item. Press Enter to search and open an authorized result. The browser's Back button returns you to your previous view.
`;var A0=`# Settings and access

## Make the workspace yours

Open **Settings** in the workspace sidebar. Choose **System**, **Light** or **Dark** for Appearance. System follows your device. On a narrow screen, use **Browse workspace** to open the sidebar.

**Show Stead Handbook in Projects** controls the Handbook entry for your account in this browser. Turn it off to shorten your project list. Return to Settings and turn it on to restore the entry. A saved Handbook link still opens while the entry is hidden.

## What other people see

The name at the top of Stead belongs to the signed-in account. The Urbit ID shown with it identifies the ship that approved the session. For a galaxy, star or planet, Stead draws that ship\u2019s sigil beside the name. Two ships can look alike at this size. Read the Urbit ID when you need to tell them apart.

Appearance applies to everyone using this browser profile. Each signed-in account chooses whether the Handbook appears in its own project list. Other projects show only when your Home has granted you access. If you expected a project, ask its maintainer to check which Urbit identity has membership.

## Sign in on the private pilot

Think of Home as the team's front door and your ship as the place where you answer, \u201CYes, that's me.\u201D On the configured pilot computer, open both sites with \`stead-pilot\`.

1. On **Stead**, enter your Urbit ID and request sign-in. Home gives this browser a short-lived request and displays a comparison code.
2. On **Sign-in approval**, sign in to your own ship if prompted. Check that the Home address and the comparison code match what you see in Stead. Choose **Approve sign-in**. Your ship sends its approval directly to Home.
3. Return to the original Stead tab and choose **Open Stead** or **Check approval**. Home checks that the approval came from the ship on your current membership and that this is the browser that started the request. It uses that approval once and gives this browser a Stead session.

Your Urbit ID answers \u201CWho is this?\u201D Your project role answers \u201CWhat can they do here?\u201D A reader can open a shared page. A contributor can save an edit where the project allows it. Knowing someone\u2019s ship name or comparison code alone gives you no project access.

The private tunnel gets this computer to the pilot. Sign-in establishes your Stead session. Git asks for its own repository credential when you clone or fetch. Keep your ship login code on your own ship\u2019s login page. **Sign out** in Stead ends the Home session. The approval site has its own sign-out.
`;var D0=`# Give an agent a task

Start with a Work item, a specific deliverable and a person who can judge the result. A document edit is a good first task. An agent can help in a project that has no code repository.

## Write a brief someone can review

The Handbook\u2019s **Update the visitor guide** task asks, \u201CMove the opening meeting from Monday to Wednesday in the visitor guide. Keep the time, meeting point and all other wording. Show the proposed change for review.\u201D

Link or identify the exact page, describe what the agent may change, and say when it should ask a question. For this example, it should ask if the page contains two different Monday meetings.

## Choose the worker

The Owner is the person accountable for the outcome. On a Home with registered agent workers, **Working on it** identifies the worker. The current private pilot shows **Assignee**. Assigning a name or copying the brief does not start a model. If your Home has no connected agent runner, the brief is still usable for a manual handoff.

## Does each agent need a moon?

No. A moon gives a worker its own ship address under a parent planet. Use one when the worker needs to communicate as its own Urbit ship. A Home can also identify a registered agent and bind it to a worker runtime. The operator connects that runtime to a local model or a compatible hosted provider. Neither a moon's parent planet nor a model name grants access to a project. [How Urbit IDs work](https://docs.urbit.org/urbit-id/what-is-urbit-id)

Grant the worker only the project, document and actions this task requires. Keep a human owner and choose a reviewer. Never share a person's ship sign-in code with a model.

## Review the result

Ask for the exact proposed edit. Compare it with the original and request a revision if anything else changed. The reviewer checks the current document and accepts the exact change. Mark the Work done after you can reopen the accepted result.

The complete assign, clarify, revise and accept journey is still being tested. A model response or a comment alone does not change the saved document.
`;var O0=`# Common questions

## Where is Code?

Look beside **Docs** in a project that has a repository you can access. General projects can use Work and Docs on their own. The Handbook's **Code** tab has a Git README you can explore.

## Can I try this without changing team work?

Yes. Open **Work** and select **Update the visitor guide**. Read the task and its discussion, then choose **Show links** under **Relationships** to open the related agent guide. In **Docs**, choose **Handbook guides** and open a page. Open **Code** and read the README beneath the file list. Select **README.md** to switch between **Read** and **Raw**. Everyone can explore this read-only project.

Choose **Show me around** for a short tour of those views. **Skip** dismisses it. **Replay tour** brings it back.

## Can I hide this project and still read an article?

Yes. **Settings** controls whether the Handbook appears in your sidebar. Keep a bookmark to any article, or restore the project from Settings.

## How do I know a change was saved?

A draft or proposed edit still needs acceptance. After saving, reopen the Work item or page and check the saved content and revision. For code, check the commit displayed by the project.

## How does the Handbook stay current?

The guides travel with each Stead release. A maintainer can change the Markdown alongside the feature it explains. Everyone running that release reads the same version, including someone who hid the Handbook and opens it again later.
